{"records":[{"id":"3kkc9yp8k7","title":"Mouse fetal liver/bone marrow transduced with NOTCH1-dE","description":"Acute leukemias are aggressive malignancies of developmentally arrested hematopoietic progenitors. We sought here to explore the possibility that changes in hematopoietic stem/progenitor cells during development might alter the biology of leukemias arising from this tissue compartment. Using a mouse model of acute T-cell leukemia, we found that leukemias generated from fetal liver (FL) and adult bone marrow (BM) differed dramatically in their leukemia stem cell activity with FL leukemias showing markedly reduced serial transplantability as compared to BM leukemias. We present evidence that this difference is due to NOTCH1-driven autocrine IGF1 signaling which is active in FL cells, but restrained in BM cells by EZH2-dependent H3K27 trimethylation. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-09-27T15:16:13.355Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3kkc9yp8k7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3kkc9yp8k7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Andrew","last_name":"Weng","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Fetal Development","Insulin-Like Growth Factor","Aging","T Cell","Lymphoid Malignancy","Notch","Mouse Model of Cancer","Transduction of Hematopoietic Stem Cells","Acute Leukemia"],"journal_issn":["19345909"]},{"id":"774twr6fb8","title":"Precision Epitope-engineering in Hematopoietic Stem and Progenitor Cells enables CD123 CAR-T cell immunotherapy for acute myeloid leukemia_1","description":"This dataset contains the raw FACS data of in vitro experiments used to generate the Figures in the paper titled \"Precision Epitope-engineering in Hematopoietic Stem and Progenitor Cells enables CD123 CAR-T cell immunotherapy for acute myeloid leukemia\".\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-08-20T06:12:09.566Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/774twr6fb8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/774twr6fb8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"ying","last_name":"zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ruijin","last_name":"Ji","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Hematopoietic Stem Cell","Acute Leukemia"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7tzk9r4mn8","title":"Precision Epitope-engineering in Hematopoietic Stem and Progenitor Cells enables CD123 CAR-T cell immunotherapy for acute myeloid leukemia_2","description":"This dataset contains the raw FACS data of in vivo experiments without CART or AML used to generate the Figures in the paper titled \"Precision Epitope-engineering in Hematopoietic Stem and Progenitor Cells enables CD123 CAR-T cell immunotherapy for acute myeloid leukemia\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-08-20T06:12:09.186Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7tzk9r4mn8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7tzk9r4mn8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"ying","last_name":"zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ruijin","last_name":"Ji","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Hematopoietic Stem Cell","Acute Leukemia"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"kd9bjf244c","title":"Precision Epitope-engineering in Hematopoietic Stem and Progenitor Cells enables CD123 CAR-T cell immunotherapy for acute myeloid leukemia_3","description":"This dataset contains the raw FACS data of in vivo experiments with CART or AML used to generate the Figures in the paper titled \"Precision Epitope-engineering in Hematopoietic Stem and Progenitor Cells enables CD123 CAR-T cell immunotherapy for acute myeloid leukemia\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-08-20T06:10:41.36Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/kd9bjf244c","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/kd9bjf244c"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"ying","last_name":"zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Hematopoietic Stem Cell","Acute Leukemia"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cnv5p5rszz","title":"CHOP Deficiency Enhances Hematopoietic Stem Cell Function via Reducing ATF3/ROS Induced Cell Apoptosis","description":"Hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs) reside in a quiescent niche to reserve their capacity of self-renewal. Upon hematopoietic injuries, HSCs enter the cell cycle and encounter protein homeostasis problems caused by accumulation of mis-folded proteins. However, the mechanism by which protein homeostasis influences HSC function and maintenance remains poorly understood. Here, we show that C/EBP homologous protein (CHOP), which is demonstrated previously, induces cell death upon tunicamycin induced unfolded protein response (UPR), plays an important role in HSCs regeneration. CHOP-/- mice showed normal hematopoietic stem and progenitor cell frequencies in steady state. However, when treated with 5-FU, CHOP deficiency resulted in higher survival rates, associated with an increased number of HSCs and reduced level of apoptosis. In serial competitive transplantation experiments, CHOP-/- HSCs showed a dramatic enhancement of repopulation ability and a reduction of protein aggresomes. Mechanistically, CHOP deletion causes reduced ATF3 expression and further leads to decreased protein aggregation and ROS. In addition, CHOP-/- HSCs exhibited an increased resistance to IR-induced DNA damage and improved HSCs homeostasis and function in telomere-dysfunctional (G3Terc-/-) mice. In summary, these findings disclose a new role of CHOP in the regulation of the HSCs function and homeostasis through reducing ATF3 and ROS signaling.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-08-27T04:57:54.562Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cnv5p5rszz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cnv5p5rszz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"zhencan","last_name":"shi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Blood Flow","Chip Formation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pbbn55mhy9","title":"Data for \"Identification of a retinoic acid-dependent hemogenic endothelial progenitor from human pluripotent stem cells\"","description":"The generation of hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs) from human pluripotent stem cells (hPSCs) is a major goal for regenerative medicine. During embryonic development, HSCs derive from hemogenic endothelium (HE) in a NOTCH and retinoic acid (RA)-dependent manner. While a WNT-dependent (WNTd) patterning of nascent hPSC mesoderm specifies clonally multipotent intra-embryonic-like HOXA+ definitive HE, this HE is functionally unresponsive to RA. Here we show that WNTd mesoderm, prior to HE specification, is actually comprised of two distinct KDR+CD34neg populations. CXCR4negCYP26A1+ mesoderm gives rise to HOXA+ multilineage definitive HE in an RA-independent manner, while CXCR4+ALDH1A2+ mesoderm gives rise to HOXA+ multilineage definitive HE in a stage-specific, RA-dependent manner. Further, both RA-independent (RAi) and -dependent (RAd) HE harbor transcriptional similarity to distinct populations found in the early human embryo, including HSC-competent HE. This revised model of human hematopoietic development provides essential resolution to the regulation and origins of the multiple waves of hematopoiesis. These insights provide the basis for the generation of specific hematopoietic populations, including the de novo specification of HSCs. ","source":{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-02-16T15:36:31.636Z","url":"https://ordr.hsr.it/datasets/pbbn55mhy9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pbbn55mhy9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Andrea","last_name":"Ditadi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stephanie","last_name":"Luff","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christopher","last_name":"Sturgeon","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Philip","last_name":"Creamer","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sara","last_name":"Valsoni","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Carissa","last_name":"Dege","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rebecca","last_name":"Scarfò","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Analisa","last_name":"Dacunto","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sara","last_name":"Cascione","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lauren","last_name":"Randolph","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Eleonora","last_name":"Cavalca","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ivan","last_name":"Merelli","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Samantha","last_name":"Morris","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Retinoic Acid","Hematopoietic Development","Mesoderm","Pluripotent Stem Cell Differentiation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"25v78pp9pg","title":"Hematopoietic reconstitution dynamics of mobilized- and bone marrow-derived human hematopoietic stem cells after gene therapy","description":"Mobilized peripheral blood is increasingly used instead of bone marrow as a source of autologous hematopoietic stem/progenitor cells for ex vivo gene therapy. Here, we present an unplanned exploratory analysis evaluating the hematopoietic reconstitution kinetics, engraftment and clonality in 13 pediatric Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome patients treated with autologous lentiviral-vector transduced hematopoietic stem/progenitor cells derived from mobilized peripheral blood (n=7), bone marrow (n=5) or the combination of the two sources (n=1). 8 out of 13 gene therapy patients were enrolled in an open-label, non-randomized, phase 1/2 clinical study(NCT01515462) and the remaining 5 patients were treated under expanded access programs. Although mobilized peripheral blood- and bone marrow- hematopoietic stem/progenitor cells display similar capability of being gene-corrected, maintaining the engineered grafts up to 3 years after gene therapy, mobilized peripheral blood-gene therapy group shows faster neutrophil and platelet recovery, higher number of engrafted clones and increased gene correction in the myeloid lineage which correlate with higher amount of primitive and myeloid progenitors contained in hematopoietic stem/progenitor cells derived from mobilized peripheral blood. In vitro differentiation and transplantation studies in mice confirm that primitive hematopoietic stem/progenitor cells from both sources have comparable engraftment and multilineage differentiation potential. Altogether, our analyses reveal that the differential behavior after gene therapy of hematopoietic stem/progenitor cells derived from either bone marrow or mobilized peripheral blood is mainly due to the distinct cell composition rather than functional differences of the infused cell products, providing new frames of references for clinical interpretation of hematopoietic stem/progenitor cell transplantation outcome.\n Data supporting the current study are part of a registered clinical trial (NCT01515462).  These data are available under restricted access for the sensitive nature of the clinical data, access can be obtained by request to the corresponding author (Prof. Alessandro Aiuti, San Raffaele Telethon Institute for Gene Therapy (SR-TIGET), IRCCS San Raffaele Scientific Institute, Milan, 20132 Italy.). We intend to reply to any requests within two weeks and we will share the deposited data only for research purposes. The non-clinical data generated in this study are also provided as the Source Data file associated to the published work.","source":{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-07-14T13:41:18.256Z","url":"https://ordr.hsr.it/datasets/25v78pp9pg","data_type":"dataset","access":"restricted","doi":["10.17632/25v78pp9pg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alessandro","last_name":"Aiuti","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Serena","last_name":"Scala","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/04xraxn18","name":"Telethon Foundation","grant_id":"TIGET Core Grant B2"},{"id":"https://ror.org/03zcxha54","name":"Else Kröner-Fresenius-Stiftung","grant_id":"Prize to Alessandro Aiuti"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","grant_id":"ERARE-3-JTC2015"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00789fa95","name":"Ministero della Salute","grant_id":"NET-2011-02350069"}],"categories":["Gene Therapy","Bone Marrow Stem Cell","Hematopoietic Stem Cell","Stem Cell Mobilization"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hx4nwr8yyd","title":"Data for: Therapy-related acute myeloid leukemia developing 14 years after allogeneic stem cell transplantation, from a persistent R882H-DNMT3A mutated clone of patient origin.","description":"Supplementary (DOCX  2.34MB): \nMaterials and methods. \nFig. S1. TP53 mutation found in the lung carcinoma cells taken from the patient in 2012. \nFig. S2. Karyotype illustrating the metaphases of the 2015 therapy-related myelodysplastic syndrome cells. \nFig. S3. Paint of chromosome 15 (green) by fluorescent in situ hybridization. \nFig. S4. Analysis of the chimerism (Mentype® Chimera® software, Biotype) at 2015 therapy-related acute myeloid leukemia diagnosis. \nFig. S5. IDH1, DNMT3A and NPM1 mutations found at the time of the acute myeloid leukemia diagnosis in 2001. \nFig. S6. DNMT3A and TP53 mutations found at the time of the therapy-related acute myeloid leukemia diagnosis in 2015. \nFig. S7. Standard curve for the ASO-qPCR. \nFig. S8. Sensitivity assay for quantitative ASO-qPCR. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-07-18T12:28:12.179Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hx4nwr8yyd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hx4nwr8yyd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Iván","last_name":"Martín","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Felipe J","last_name":"Chaves","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rosario","last_name":"Abellán","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Francisca","last_name":"García","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mar","last_name":"Tormo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Carlos","last_name":"Solano","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Juan-Carlos","last_name":"Hernandez-Boluda","orcid":null},{"first_name":"María Dolores","last_name":"Olivares","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Eva","last_name":"Villamón","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fernando","last_name":"Domingo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Blanca","last_name":"Navarro","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marisa","last_name":"Calabuig","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Paula","last_name":"Amat","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alicia","last_name":"Serrano","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Cytogenetics"],"journal_issn":["00144800"]},{"id":"ztwnydbhsw","title":"G2 arrest primes hematopoietic stem cells for megakaryopoiesis","description":"In contrast to most hematopoietic lineages, megakaryocytes (MKs) can derive rapidly and directly from hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs). The underlying mechanism is unclear however. Here we show that DNA damage induces MK markers in HSCs and that G2 arrest, an integral part of the DNA damage response, suffices for MK priming followed by irreversible MK differentiation in HSCs, but not in progenitors. We also show that replication stress causes DNA damage in HSCs and is at least in part due to uracil misincorporation in vitro and in vivo.  Consistent with this notion, thymidine attenuated DNA damage, improved HSC maintenance and reduced the generation of CD41+ MK-committed HSCs. Replication stress and concomitant MK differentiation is therefore one of the barriers to HSC maintenance. DNA damage-induced MK priming may allow rapid generation of a lineage essential to immediate organismal survival, while also removing damaged cells from the HSC pool. \nThis dataset accompanies a manuscript in Cell Report, 2024, and contain the data from ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-06-05T06:57:20.621Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ztwnydbhsw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ztwnydbhsw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hans","last_name":"Snoeck","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Cycle","DNA Damage Response","Hematopoietic Stem Cell","General Hematopoiesis","Megakaryocytopoiesis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bgbspx3mxw","title":"Distinct and interacting roles of Mitofusins in hematopoiesis","description":"Data for all figures and supplementary figures for a submitted manuscript. In this manuscript we described the role of mitofusins in hematopoiesis. We previously reported that the mitochondrial fusion mediator, mitofusin (Mfn)2, promotes maintenance of lymphoid-biased hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs). Here, we demonstrate that combined loss in the hematopoietic system of both Mfn homologues, Mfn1 and Mfn2, causes embryonic lethality, cytopenias, as well as long-term and short-term reconstitution failure after transplantation. A single Mfn1 allele rescued these defects. However, mice with a single Mfn2 allele showed impaired erythroid, B and T cell development. Mfn deletion caused abnormal regulation of mitochondrial mass in erythroid progenitors, a hyperenergetic state, and evidence of mitochondrial stress. Combined Mfn deletion in the lymphoid lineage was sufficient to impair lymphopoiesis, while ex vivo induction of mitochondrial stress in WT cells primarily impaired lymphoid progenitors, indicating mitochondrial stress as the primary driver of the lymphoid defects. Mfns therefore play only partially redundant and lineage-specific roles in hematopoiesis through maintenance of mass, prevention of hypermetabolism and mitigation of mitochondrial stress. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-02-02T19:54:10.558Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bgbspx3mxw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bgbspx3mxw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hans","last_name":"Snoeck","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/012pb6c26","name":"National Heart Lung and Blood Institute","grant_id":"R01HL135039"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00fj8a872","name":"Office of the Director","grant_id":"S10 OD032447 "},{"id":"https://ror.org/00fj8a872","name":"Office of the Director","grant_id":"S10OD020056"}],"categories":["Mitochondrial Function","Hematopoietic Stem Cell","General Hematopoiesis"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":135425,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":150,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":135425,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":134728,"restricted":67,"embargoed":630},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2270},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1337},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":663},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":473},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":441},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":418},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":406},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":382},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":323},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":274}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":132792},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1901},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":185},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":136},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":77},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":65},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":56},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":40},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":871},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":769},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":518},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":405},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":360}]}}